      "subtitle": "The four verbs you'll actually use every day",
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "title": "ser — permanent 'to be'",
          "note": "Identity, origin, profession. soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son. Read it aloud: the soy/eres/es/son forms are also the present tense of ir.",
          "verbIds": ["ser"],
          "focusPersonae": ["yo", "tu", "el", "ellos"]
        },
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "title": "estar — temporary 'to be'",
          "note": "Location, mood, condition. estar patterns like -er, not -ar, even though it ends in -ar.",
          "verbIds": ["estar"],
          "focusPersonae": ["yo", "tu", "el", "ellos"]
        },
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "title": "ir — to go",
          "note": "Fully irregular: voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van.",
          "verbIds": ["ir"],
          "focusPersonae": ["yo", "tu", "el"]
        },
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "title": "tener — to have",
          "note": "Only the yo form breaks: tengo. Everything else is regular -er.",
          "verbIds": ["tener"],
          "focusPersonae": ["yo"]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "title": "ser vs estar",
          "body": "The single hardest pair in Spanish:\n\nser → who you are. Soy feliz. (I am happy — permanently.)\nestar → where you are. Estoy feliz. (I am happy — today.)\n\nSame sentence, two meanings. If swapping the verb changes the meaning, use estar.\n\nSo: Soy en Londres. (I live in London.) Estoy en Londres. (I am in London right now.)"
        }
      ],
